  {
   "id": "YdEitX5WP_kJ",
   "from": "Assaf Mendelson",
   "author": "b8c77fa5305c80cb",
   "date": "2013-07-21T13:29:46Z",
   "subject": "Re: [Israel RUG] Basic string manipulation",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "I think you meant regexec, if I use regexpr it just gives everything.<br><br><br>I ended up implementing a quick utility function to solve this:<br><br># Function to perform sscanf with %d values only.<br># input: text: The string to parse<br># input: pattern: a sscanf style pattern (currently only %d is supported)<br># output: List with two members: res$length contains the number of elements found, <br>#                                res$vals contains a vector of the found values.<br>sscanf_numbers &lt;- function(text,pattern) {<br>    # replaces all occurances of %d with regular expression equivalent (all numbers). Must be surrounded by<br>    # () to allow regexec (below) to know it is something to extract<br>    pattern=gsub(&quot;%d&quot;,&quot;([0-9]*)&quot;,pattern);<br>    # concatinates with regex begining/end <br>    pattern=paste(&quot;^&quot;,pattern,&quot;$&quot;,sep=&quot;&quot;);<br>    # make the extraction<br>    m = regexec(pattern,text);<br>    m = regmatches(text,m);<br>    # The result would be a list of vectors where each vector contains all matches (starting with the <br>    # overall and an additional match for every expression in &#39;()&#39;).<br>    # so we want to get a vector of everything EXCEPT the first match (if no match then that would be the first)<br>    # element and we would get nothing.<br>    convert_to_result&lt;-function(single_element) {<br>        single_element = single_element[-1]; <br>        res = list(length(single_element),as.integer(single_element)); <br>        names(res)&lt;-c(&quot;length&quot;,&quot;vals&quot;); <br>        return(res)<br>    }<br>    res = lapply(m,convert_to_result)<br>    return(res);"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<br>}<br><br>On Sunday, July 21, 2013 4:05:56 PM UTC+3, Jonathan Rosenblatt wrote:"
